president biden signing three new  executive orders today all related to  immigration this is the second time the  president  has focused on that topic since taking  office  christian kazar is joining us live from  our 8 news now newsroom  on what this means christian that's  right brian well president biden focused  on three areas in his executive orders  the unification of families separated  at the border a review of the asylum  policies and a reverse  on the previous administration's  immigration agenda three areas that we  know will not cause immediate change now  let's start with the task force which  will focus on reuniting the estimated  600 plus  children that were separated two years  ago from their families along the border  during the previous administration many  of those parents  were deported to mexico and central  america president biden called this  the national shame of the previous  administration's immigration policies  mr biden also will review trump's  administration's  remaining mexico policy which required  about 700 000 asylum seekers to wait  outside the u.s territory  while their cases were educated making  sure  that he's actually keeping promises that  he made  during the campaign trial and i think  that's very very important i think one  of the most important  policies for me right now is the fact  that he is going to develop  that task force to make sure that he's  reuniting those families that were  actually separated  um over under the trump administration  well the white house officials say the  reuniting of families will be a  case-by-case basis separated families  could be  eligible for parole visas or other  immigration benefits to enable  their reunification now again  the immediate immediate change according  to white house  will not take any sort of immediate  effect but local pro-immigrant groups  that we talked to  are very hopeful about the direction the  president is heading it's only been a  few weeks and they say it seems  immigration is obviously a top priority  for the white house  we'll be back after this break
 

